{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":5,"delta":"{\"i\":\"Writing migration report\",\"path\":\"<RUNNER_ROOT>/results/hb-devhard-043-gpt54-medium-host4-r1/trials/009-omp-attempt-003/hb-work/omp/gpt-5.4/oc-bench-v2-043-db-migration-safety-gpt-5.4-20260821-140005-9ffb77c6/workspace/in/db/migration_report.md\",\"content\":\"# Migration report\\n\\n## Strategy\\n\\n- Run offline in SQLite inside an explicit `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction.\\n- Temporarily disable foreign-key enforcement during the table rebuild, then restore it and run `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`.\\n- Rebuild `users` instead of dropping data in place.\\n- Preserve every user id, every order row, every `orders.user_id` reference, and every historical `created_at` value.\\n\\n## Dirty-data cleanup\\n\\nThe migration rewrites only the known dirty emails from the preflight dataset:\\n\\n- `u4` -> `ada+u4@example.com`\\n- `u5` -> `missing+u5@example.invalid`\\n- `u6` -> `missing+u6@example.invalid`\\n\\n`u1` remains the canonical `ada@example.com` row. All other user emails are copied through unchanged.\\n\\n## Target constraints\\n\\nThe rebuilt `users` table enforces:\\n\\n- `status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'`\\n- `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE`\\n- `CHECK (LENGTH(TRIM(email)) > 0)` to reject blank future writes in addition to nulls and duplicates\\n\\n## Idempotency approach\\n\\nThe migration is deterministic. Re-running it rebuilds `users` from the current table contents, reapplies the same cleanup mapping for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, and preserves ids, orders, and `created_at` values without duplicating rows.\\n\\n## Rollback behavior and limitation\\n\\n- `rollback.sql` rebuilds `users` back to the old schema shape: `id`, `email`, `name`, `created_at`.\\n- It preserves user-row count, order-row count, order references, and current email values.\\n- Limitation: rollback removes the `status` column and relaxed constraints, but it does not recover the original dirty emails once cleanup has been applied.\\n\\n## Postcheck queries to run\\n\\nExecute:\\n\\n```sql\\n.read in/db/postcheck.sql\\n```\\n\\nThe postcheck verifies:\\n\\n- user and order row counts\\n- preserved dirty-user order references (`u4`, `u5`, `u6`)\\n- exact cleaned email values for `u4`, `u5`, `u6`\\n- non-null, non-blank, unique email outcomes\\n- `status` column metadata and default\\n- unique-email index presence\\n- foreign-key integrity\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":5,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_YwInaB9W6iNJoKMztJDH7Ij1|fc_0b2744c22e5ad7c6016a8892be395487d192b25005550e3704","name":"write","arguments":{"i":"Writing migration report","path":"<RUNNER_ROOT>/results/hb-devhard-043-gpt54-medium-host4-r1/trials/009-omp-attempt-003/hb-work/omp/gpt-5.4/oc-bench-v2-043-db-migration-safety-gpt-5.4-20260821-140005-9ffb77c6/workspace/in/db/migration_report.md","content":"# Migration report\n\n## Strategy\n\n- Run offline in SQLite inside an explicit `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction.\n- Temporarily disable foreign-key enforcement during the table rebuild, then restore it and run `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`.\n- Rebuild `users` instead of dropping data in place.\n- Preserve every user id, every order row, every `orders.user_id` reference, and every historical `created_at` value.\n\n## Dirty-data cleanup\n\nThe migration rewrites only the known dirty emails from the preflight dataset:\n\n- `u4` -> `ada+u4@example.com`\n- `u5` -> `missing+u5@example.invalid`\n- `u6` -> `missing+u6@example.invalid`\n\n`u1` remains the canonical `ada@example.com` row. All other user emails are copied through unchanged.\n\n## Target constraints\n\nThe rebuilt `users` table enforces:\n\n- `status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'`\n- `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE`\n- `CHECK (LENGTH(TRIM(email)) > 0)` to reject blank future writes in addition to nulls and duplicates\n\n## Idempotency approach\n\nThe migration is deterministic. Re-running it rebuilds `users` from the current table contents, reapplies the same cleanup mapping for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, and preserves ids, orders, and `created_at` values without duplicating rows.\n\n## Rollback behavior and limitation\n\n- `rollback.sql` rebuilds `users` back to the old schema shape: `id`, `email`, `name`, `created_at`.\n- It preserves user-row count, order-row count, order references, and current email values.\n- Limitation: rollback removes the `status` column and relaxed constraints, but it does not recover the original dirty emails once cleanup has been applied.\n\n## Postcheck queries to run\n\nExecute:\n\n```sql\n.read in/db/postcheck.sql\n```\n\nThe postcheck verifies:\n\n- user and order row counts\n- preserved dirty-user order references (`u4`, `u5`, `u6`)\n- exact cleaned email values for `u4`, `u5`, `u6`\n- non-null, non-blank, unique email outcomes\n- `status` column metadata and default\n- unique-email index presence\n- foreign-key integrity"}}}}
